comments[10]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-29T04:47:10.315Z","harness:claude-code","Lexicon census 2026-07-29 giving this item a concrete acceptance target. The live surface is 84 commands and 1,792 flag occurrences drawn from 481 distinct spellings. Two hundred spellings, 41.6 percent, appear on exactly one command; 54 appear on ten or more; the shared head is --help 84, --author 33, --limit 30, --message 30, --force 27, --priority 26, --parent 24, --type 24, --sprint 23, --release 23, --assignee 22, --tag 20. Two hundred and two spellings cover 80 percent of occurrences, so the tail is long rather than negligible. Per-command flag budgets, which this item's title already promises, now have real outliers to bound: update-many 126, plan 97, update 84, create 78, and five list-family commands at 63 to 65 each. The strongest single finding for the budget rule is that create, update and update-many spend 288 occurrences, sixteen percent of the entire surface, re-declaring one item schema three times with divergent arity - that is the population a one-spelling-per-concept gate should collapse first, and it is measurable before and after. The second is that a singleton flag is indistinguishable from a shared one in the rendered surface, so the gate needs a declared local-or-shared disposition per flag rather than a similarity heuristic. Sequencing consequence recorded as ruling F on pm-pbyu: this item is upstream of the noun consolidation, not parallel to it."
  "2026-07-29T16:56:19.651Z","harness:claude-code","A concrete two-concept collision for the lexicon, confirmed from live help at build 2026.7.29.\n\nThe flag --file carries two incompatible meanings depending on the command:\n\nOn create and update, --file is item data. The value is a linked file path, in the form path=<value>,scope=<project|global>,note=<text>. The value is stored on the item.\n\nOn comments, notes and learnings, --file is an input channel. The value is a path the command reads the prose payload out of. The value is not stored anywhere; only the file's contents are.\n\nBoth meanings are load-bearing and both are the documented recommendation for their own family - the linked-file form because a bare colon-separated string stored through update --file is a known source of missing-path drift, and the read-from-file form because prose passed as a shell argument silently loses backtick-quoted content to command substitution. An agent that generalises one meaning to the other corrupts data in one direction and stores prose as a path in the other, and no error is raised in either case.\n\nThis is a clean test for the first criterion here: a lexicon that maps every flag to exactly one concept must split this spelling into two concepts with two canonical spellings, and the third criterion is satisfiable because the two meanings live on disjoint command sets, so both current spellings can survive as hidden aliases with no ambiguity.\n"
  "2026-08-01T09:52:04.266Z","harness:claude-code","Flag-budget evidence, measured 2026-08-01 at HEAD. pm create is cited in this item's description as publishing more than sixty flags; pm list publishes 69, which is the larger surface and the one an agent reaches first. Of those 69, 22 are presence predicates in three interchangeable spellings for the same concept -- --has-notes/--no-notes, --no-files/--filter-files-missing, --filter-ac-missing, --empty-body/--has-body -- so a single declared predicate grammar (pm-plwm's --where) would retire roughly a third of the command's flag surface without removing a capability. Also measured: the full per-command help surface is 218,872 bytes across 88 command spellings, about 54,718 tokens, against 9,701 bytes for pm contracts --summary. The flag surface is where that 54,718 lives."
  "2026-08-07T11:23:41.988Z","harness:claude-code","Evidence measured 2026-08-07 for the one-concept-per-flag clause. Two live violations. First, --full denotes four different concepts: include heavy schema sections (contracts), the complete dependency row projection (deps), the complete graph row projection (graph), and expanded history entries (history). The value domain is boolean in every case, so the value-domain rule alone does not catch it — the lexicon needs the concept identity clause it already declares. Second, output bounding is spelled five ways across the surface: --output-limit and --output-budget as universal controls, and --limit, --node-limit, --edge-limit and --token-budget as per-command spellings, with deps carrying four of them privately. Recorded as evidence for the flag budget and the published lexicon."
  "2026-08-18T19:18:03.669Z","harness:codex","Implementation start 2026-08-18: active in one coupled SDK-first command-contract tranche spanning canonical flag concepts and budgets, truthful invocation arity, complete closed-domain recovery, refusal-closure scoring, source-derived executable recovery obligations, and shared CLI/MCP/completion/docs discovery metadata. This item is claimed because its acceptance surface will be edited and verified in this branch; unrelated parents and backlog items remain open and unclaimed."
  "2026-08-18T20:46:15.932Z","harness:codex","Verification evidence: canonical lexicon now contains 3,043 entries across 70 persisted no-growth command budgets; aliases do not consume canonical budget; exact repository coverage passes at 100/100/100/100 across 7,581 tests."
  "2026-08-18T21:13:35.522Z","harness:codex","Tranche evidence: published SDK lexicon data now exposes 3,043 canonical entries and 70 persisted no-growth command budgets; compatibility aliases do not consume canonical budgets, collision/value-kind/budget negative controls fail closed, generated docs and command-grammar gates are current, and linked verification passed. Remaining acceptance: classify semantic synonym concepts rather than spelling-derived concepts, prove every pre-change spelling through an explicit inventory, and record per-command before/after token deltas. Returning open and releasing ownership."
  "2026-08-18T23:41:25.579Z","harness:codex","Exact-head review evidence: command budgets now derive current counts from the canonical lexicon instead of copying maxima; dash/underscore spellings merge into one canonical row while preserving the raw alias; stale budget families render as unknown instead of throwing. Focused regressions and the final 529-file / 7,583-test suite pass at exact 100/100/100/100."
  "2026-08-19T01:01:53.045Z","harness:codex","Verification evidence 2026-08-19: review amendments preserve every executable root/local option observation and fail on conflicting arity except the documented Commander local-author shadowing contract; flag-lexicon findings sort deterministically by command/code/detail; refusal success requires usage exit. Final conformance reports 2,831 declared and 2,831 observed flags with zero findings, 3,043 lexicon entries across 70 budgets with zero findings, 166 command paths, and exact 100/100/100/100 coverage across 7,586 tests. Remaining acceptance is still semantic synonym classification, explicit full pre-change spelling inventory, and per-command help-token deltas, so this item will return open."
  "2026-08-19T01:42:43.198Z","harness:codex","Review-amendment evidence: strengthened retained gate negative-control validation, omitted empty merged alias arrays, memoized the immutable canonical lexicon, pinned the budget negative control to the observed corpus, and reconciled pm-pcy92t's initial 2904 raw inventory against the final 2831 canonical rows. Focused tests passed 14/14; full coverage passed 529/529 files and 7587/7587 tests with exact 100% statements (55445/55445), branches (41368/41368), functions (11473/11473), and lines (53501/53501); repository static quality, contracts, command grammar, 2831/2831 invocation parity, 3043-entry lexicon, 8/8 refusal closure, gate registry, SDK surface, graph composition, and record integrity all passed."
notes[1]{created_at,author,text}:
  "2026-07-25T17:54:06.791Z","harness:claude-code","Flag-axis baseline measured 2026-07-25 (shipped 2026.7.25), for the per-command flag budget this item declares.\n\n1,712 flag declarations across 77 top-level commands; 470 distinct spellings. Distribution is extremely skewed — the top five commands (plan 97, update 82, create 78, list 66, search 58) carry 381 declarations, 22% of the total on 6% of the commands.\n\nTwo candidate budget shapes the item should choose between, with evidence:\n- A flat per-command ceiling would immediately fail on plan/update/create/list/search, which are the five commands agents use most; a ceiling low enough to be meaningful elsewhere would force real capability into subcommands or config on exactly the wrong surfaces.\n- A tiered ceiling keyed to the declared surface tier (pm-kxci8x) fits the measured shape better: core-tier commands get a hard, small budget; specialist commands are allowed a larger one but must justify each flag against the shared lexicon.\n\nLexicon evidence for the \"one spelling per concept\" half: --author appears on 35 commands, --message on 30, --compact on 28, --force on 27, --limit on 25, --priority on 23, --parent on 23, --type on 21. High repetition is the healthy case — it means the concept is shared. The conformance risk is the long tail: 470 distinct spellings against roughly 77 commands means most spellings appear once or twice, and single-use spellings are where synonym drift hides. Recommend the gate measure the tail (spellings used on exactly one command) rather than the head."
